WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.573 --> 00:00:01.498
(swooshing)

00:00:01.498 --> 00:00:03.225
(explosion)

00:00:03.225 --> 00:00:04.088
- Hello, Believe Nation.

00:00:04.088 --> 00:00:07.025
I started the Mentor Me series
to try to hang around people

00:00:07.025 --> 00:00:08.903
who've done a lot more than us,

00:00:08.903 --> 00:00:11.658
and hopefully, by spending a
little bit more time with them,

00:00:11.658 --> 00:00:14.280
some of how they think,
their values, their beliefs,

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.007
their mindsets, their
attitudes, seeps into us

00:00:17.007 --> 00:00:20.242
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:20.242 --> 00:00:21.141
So, today we're going to learn

00:00:21.141 --> 00:00:23.018
from Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson

00:00:23.018 --> 00:00:25.474
and some of his best motivation for you.

00:00:25.474 --> 00:00:26.498
Mentor me, Rock.

00:00:26.498 --> 00:00:27.828
And as always, guys, as you're watching,

00:00:27.828 --> 00:00:30.360
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.225
please leave it down in the
comments below or put quotes

00:00:32.225 --> 00:00:34.632
around it so other people
can be inspired as well.

00:00:34.632 --> 00:00:36.779
And, when you write it down,
it's much more likely to

00:00:36.779 --> 00:00:39.277
stick with yourself as well.

00:00:39.277 --> 00:00:40.110
Enjoy.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.693
(upbeat tempo)

00:00:53.021 --> 00:00:56.501
- They say that nothing good
happens in the 4:00 a.m. hour.

00:00:56.501 --> 00:00:58.563
Well, I can guaran-damn-tee you this,

00:00:58.563 --> 00:01:01.264
it's 4:45 a.m., it's still
dark outside, look at that,

00:01:01.264 --> 00:01:03.129
the lights just went out
and I'm getting ready to

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:04.090
kill this a.m. cardio.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.555
You can't even see me but
it's going to be so good,

00:01:06.555 --> 00:01:07.963
it's bad.

00:01:07.963 --> 00:01:09.907
I did a lot of my growing up in Hawaii,

00:01:09.907 --> 00:01:12.815
and all my years of struggling, as family,

00:01:12.815 --> 00:01:14.176
I was evicted out of Hawaii,

00:01:14.176 --> 00:01:17.185
we were evicted out of
our little efficiency,

00:01:17.185 --> 00:01:19.177
forced to move, forced to leave Hawaii.

00:01:19.177 --> 00:01:22.153
My grandfather, years ago,

00:01:22.153 --> 00:01:25.349
bought the wrestling promotion

00:01:25.349 --> 00:01:26.182
in Hawaii

00:01:27.683 --> 00:01:29.338
and

00:01:29.338 --> 00:01:30.995
he struggled with it.

00:01:30.995 --> 00:01:32.114
He died struggling.

00:01:32.114 --> 00:01:34.593
My grandmother took over
the wrestling promotion.

00:01:34.593 --> 00:01:38.482
She became the first woman
promoter in pro wrestling

00:01:38.482 --> 00:01:42.690
in a very cut-throat,
shark infested world.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.343
And, anyway, Hawaii just always

00:01:45.343 --> 00:01:47.723
represented so much struggle.

00:01:47.723 --> 00:01:50.326
I started getting molested
when I was 14 years old,

00:01:50.326 --> 00:01:51.199
doing a lot of stupid (silent cursing)

00:01:51.199 --> 00:01:52.939
that I shouldn't have been doing.

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:55.994
I was, at that time, when we got evicted

00:01:55.994 --> 00:01:57.416
out of our efficiency,

00:01:57.416 --> 00:01:59.391
I thought it was the worst
thing that could ever happen,

00:01:59.391 --> 00:02:00.982
and actually, at that time, it was.

00:02:00.982 --> 00:02:02.325
But now, in hindsight,

00:02:02.325 --> 00:02:04.113
it was the greatest thing to ever happen

00:02:04.113 --> 00:02:07.298
because it forced me into
this mindset, honestly,

00:02:07.298 --> 00:02:10.642
of hard work, because I was
like, if I could do anything,

00:02:10.642 --> 00:02:12.439
I would do everything I can to make sure

00:02:12.439 --> 00:02:14.298
we never get evicted again.

00:02:14.298 --> 00:02:17.173
And, anyway, that kind of
mentality drives me today.

00:02:17.173 --> 00:02:18.796
Yes, I get up at crazy hours,

00:02:18.796 --> 00:02:19.942
whether it's three o'clock in the morning,

00:02:19.942 --> 00:02:21.339
by the way, when we
were talking in London,

00:02:21.339 --> 00:02:22.395
I was getting up at three

00:02:22.395 --> 00:02:24.190
because I had to be on set by four.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:25.727
I'm sorry, by seven.

00:02:25.727 --> 00:02:28.541
So, whatever time my call time is,

00:02:28.541 --> 00:02:31.833
and I tell this to studios and
directors and our producers,

00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:32.666
whatever time's my call time,

00:02:32.666 --> 00:02:34.925
so if my call time is at seven,

00:02:34.925 --> 00:02:37.512
then you back your clock up four hours

00:02:37.512 --> 00:02:38.833
and that's when I get up,

00:02:38.833 --> 00:02:39.976
and I train twice,

00:02:39.976 --> 00:02:41.979
I'll get my cardio in, have
breakfast and then I'll go

00:02:41.979 --> 00:02:43.267
hit the weights.

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:46.090
Clanging and banging, we call it.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:47.333
Jacking iron.

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:48.166
- But, like, what time do you
(Dwayne laughs)

00:02:48.166 --> 00:02:48.999
go to bed.

00:02:48.999 --> 00:02:51.078
I mean, you might shoot
really late at night.

00:02:51.078 --> 00:02:53.708
- I'm averaging on, a movie,
on average, probably about five

00:02:53.708 --> 00:02:55.207
hours of sleep.
- [Interviewer] Okay.

00:02:55.207 --> 00:02:56.573
(powerful beat)
- Alright, empty gym,

00:02:56.573 --> 00:02:58.066
the way we like it.

00:02:58.066 --> 00:02:59.273
It's Sunday.

00:02:59.273 --> 00:03:00.991
Just finished my warm-up.

00:03:00.991 --> 00:03:02.805
It is family day, it's the Lord's day,

00:03:02.805 --> 00:03:05.186
it is also leg day.

00:03:05.186 --> 00:03:07.617
So, this pain ought to be fun.

00:03:07.617 --> 00:03:08.595
(silent cursing)

00:03:08.595 --> 00:03:09.694
Happy Sunday.

00:03:09.694 --> 00:03:11.381
Alright, it's Saturday afternoon.

00:03:11.381 --> 00:03:13.234
Empty gym, the way we like it.

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:14.251
I'm tripping over here.

00:03:14.251 --> 00:03:18.249
It's leg day, which means
it's going to be sweaty,

00:03:18.249 --> 00:03:19.606
painful and fun.

00:03:19.606 --> 00:03:22.946
(loud clanging)

00:03:22.946 --> 00:03:23.779
- Focus!

00:03:29.424 --> 00:03:32.091
(loud clanging)

00:03:35.938 --> 00:03:38.123
- [Trainer] Go on, get it.

00:03:38.123 --> 00:03:38.956
(panting)

00:03:38.956 --> 00:03:39.789
Come on.

00:03:41.613 --> 00:03:43.196
Four left, come on.

00:03:46.216 --> 00:03:47.108
One more, come on.

00:03:47.108 --> 00:03:47.971
- Ah!

00:03:47.971 --> 00:03:49.388
- [Trainer] Good.

00:03:51.613 --> 00:03:52.446
- Focus!

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.684
Alright, workout number two.

00:04:03.684 --> 00:04:05.798
I'm outworking all my competition.

00:04:05.798 --> 00:04:09.317
Alright, empty gym, the way we like it.

00:04:09.317 --> 00:04:10.888
Getting ready to get after it,

00:04:10.888 --> 00:04:13.888
me and my favorite training partner.

00:04:15.617 --> 00:04:18.603
(in a Mexican tone) Let's bring it on.

00:04:18.603 --> 00:04:19.973
(laughter)

00:04:19.973 --> 00:04:20.806
Let's bring it on.

00:04:20.806 --> 00:04:24.089
I was raised in a household
where my old man was kicking

00:04:24.089 --> 00:04:26.775
the (silent cursing) out
of me at an early age,

00:04:26.775 --> 00:04:28.343
teaching me hard work and discipline.

00:04:28.343 --> 00:04:31.468
I was up before the sun with him and

00:04:31.468 --> 00:04:32.551
in wrestling,

00:04:35.113 --> 00:04:37.274
a world that I grew up in,
professional wrestling,

00:04:37.274 --> 00:04:38.760
especially in the '80s,

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:42.063
'70's, '80s, where wrestling
was a lot different

00:04:42.063 --> 00:04:43.480
than it is today,

00:04:47.274 --> 00:04:48.685
it was,

00:04:48.685 --> 00:04:49.939
there was no off seasons.

00:04:49.939 --> 00:04:51.709
Obviously, there's no
off season in wrestling,

00:04:51.709 --> 00:04:52.989
and there was no off season then

00:04:52.989 --> 00:04:55.253
and there's no off season now.

00:04:55.253 --> 00:04:58.354
So, I think, having that
mentality of no off season,

00:04:58.354 --> 00:05:02.057
and being in a constant state of training

00:05:02.057 --> 00:05:03.874
and trying to get better,

00:05:03.874 --> 00:05:04.848
and by the way, it's like, look,

00:05:04.848 --> 00:05:07.808
I've got a lot of professional
athletes who are my friends,

00:05:07.808 --> 00:05:10.576
UFC fighters to pro football players,

00:05:10.576 --> 00:05:14.806
pro soccer players,
amateur, weekend warriors.

00:05:14.806 --> 00:05:17.048
The greatest part, I
think, about training is,

00:05:17.048 --> 00:05:18.116
and I always tell this to people,

00:05:18.116 --> 00:05:20.754
is, look, you can't play football forever.

00:05:20.754 --> 00:05:22.926
I played for 10 years.

00:05:22.926 --> 00:05:24.289
You can't be in the ring forever,

00:05:24.289 --> 00:05:25.704
you can't be in the Octagon forever,

00:05:25.704 --> 00:05:28.878
you can't be in the boxing ring
forever or a football field,

00:05:28.878 --> 00:05:32.150
but you can always train and
get better and push yourself.

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:34.952
Work begets work, and I
always enjoyed working,

00:05:34.952 --> 00:05:36.635
and I love what I do in
terms of making movies,

00:05:36.635 --> 00:05:39.490
so I'm always preparing
for some sort of role

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:42.197
that keeps me lean and mean.

00:05:42.197 --> 00:05:43.358
I wanted to entertain.

00:05:43.358 --> 00:05:44.518
When I was eight years old,

00:05:44.518 --> 00:05:46.525
I thought I was a
combination of these guys.

00:05:46.525 --> 00:05:48.425
I thought I was a combination of

00:05:48.425 --> 00:05:52.142
Chuck Norris, Richard Pryor, Harrison Ford

00:05:52.142 --> 00:05:53.588
and Rocky Balboa,

00:05:53.588 --> 00:05:54.975
and Harrison Ford from Indiana Jones.

00:05:54.975 --> 00:05:57.275
So, I was like all these guys in my,

00:05:57.275 --> 00:06:00.454
oh, and Elvis Presley too,
at eight, alright? (laughs)

00:06:00.454 --> 00:06:01.301
- [Interviewer] Pretty lofty ambition.

00:06:01.301 --> 00:06:02.134
- Yes, exactly.

00:06:02.134 --> 00:06:03.743
(laughter)

00:06:03.743 --> 00:06:05.665
So, I knew at that time, when I was eight,

00:06:05.665 --> 00:06:07.344
I wanted to be an entertainer,

00:06:07.344 --> 00:06:10.235
and I loved the idea of
that and what it meant.

00:06:10.235 --> 00:06:12.458
And, when I was eight, I saw Indiana Jones

00:06:12.458 --> 00:06:14.848
for the first time and
I loved that character.

00:06:14.848 --> 00:06:16.638
Like, wow, that guy was so heroic

00:06:16.638 --> 00:06:18.718
and he kicked ass and he ...

00:06:18.718 --> 00:06:19.964
I knew I wanted to be something

00:06:19.964 --> 00:06:21.592
and it was important
to me to be something.

00:06:21.592 --> 00:06:24.602
It was important to me that I didn't fail.

00:06:24.602 --> 00:06:26.873
But, by the way, and if
I did fail, at least,

00:06:26.873 --> 00:06:29.385
what was also important was the lesson,

00:06:29.385 --> 00:06:31.440
and I didn't realize
that until I got older

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:32.861
'cause, you know, when you're in it,

00:06:32.861 --> 00:06:36.127
you're in the grind, you don't
really recognize those things

00:06:36.127 --> 00:06:38.209
when you're younger, but
I could recognize them now

00:06:38.209 --> 00:06:39.382
and the importance of them now.

00:06:39.382 --> 00:06:43.200
And, just in terms of the
drive and the determination,

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.710
a lot of it was experience too.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:47.845
You get your ass kicked, you get back up,

00:06:47.845 --> 00:06:50.340
and you put the gloves
back on and you swing away.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:51.173
Me,

00:06:52.886 --> 00:06:53.803
failing has

00:06:54.944 --> 00:06:55.777
become

00:06:57.213 --> 00:06:59.046
critical in my growth.

00:07:00.442 --> 00:07:02.223
I graduated from the University of Miami.

00:07:02.223 --> 00:07:04.575
I had a terrible senior year.

00:07:04.575 --> 00:07:06.796
I had a bad injury.

00:07:06.796 --> 00:07:10.221
I had a bad injury, and you
only get one shot as a senior,

00:07:10.221 --> 00:07:11.510
you don't come back.

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:15.042
And Warren Sapp came in
and lit the world on fire

00:07:15.042 --> 00:07:16.176
with how he played.

00:07:16.176 --> 00:07:19.415
We pushed each other, we were partners.

00:07:19.415 --> 00:07:23.873
He outplayed me, beat
me out of my position.

00:07:23.873 --> 00:07:27.089
And, I had a really
terrible year that year.

00:07:27.089 --> 00:07:30.841
Graduated, didn't get
invited to the Combine,

00:07:30.841 --> 00:07:32.829
the NFL Combine,

00:07:32.829 --> 00:07:36.514
and I didn't get drafted in the NFL,

00:07:36.514 --> 00:07:39.155
and I had one option, and
that option was the CFL.

00:07:39.155 --> 00:07:42.729
So, now, you work so hard for 10 years

00:07:42.729 --> 00:07:46.312
and draft day comes,
you only get one shot.

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:50.179
Warren Sapp gets taken in the first round.

00:07:50.179 --> 00:07:52.096
All my other teammates,

00:07:53.072 --> 00:07:55.880
who are NFL worthy, were gone.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:59.087
They're in the NFL,
signing the big contracts

00:07:59.087 --> 00:08:02.238
and I signed a contract in the CFL

00:08:02.238 --> 00:08:05.155
to make 350 bucks a week, Canadian.

00:08:08.415 --> 00:08:12.299
That lasted for three
months, then I got cut,

00:08:12.299 --> 00:08:14.299
and I got sent back home

00:08:15.151 --> 00:08:18.859
and I had to move back in with
my parents at 24 years old,

00:08:18.859 --> 00:08:22.522
after I left at 18, ready
to tackle the world.

00:08:22.522 --> 00:08:24.355
And, in my way, I did,

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:29.321
but I had fallen so flat on my face

00:08:29.321 --> 00:08:31.821
for the first time in my life.

00:08:33.235 --> 00:08:34.568
I was depressed,

00:08:36.033 --> 00:08:37.774
asking myself, why?

00:08:37.774 --> 00:08:38.992
Why, why me?

00:08:38.992 --> 00:08:39.825
How can this happen?

00:08:39.825 --> 00:08:41.395
I work so hard.

00:08:41.395 --> 00:08:44.250
Hard work pays, I always thought.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:45.726
It's not paying for me.

00:08:45.726 --> 00:08:46.595
I've got nothing.

00:08:46.595 --> 00:08:48.572
I literally had seven bucks
(laughing) in my pocket,

00:08:48.572 --> 00:08:51.154
so, my seven dollar story.

00:08:51.154 --> 00:08:52.237
That failure,

00:08:53.731 --> 00:08:55.064
rising from that

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:57.474
to say

00:08:57.474 --> 00:08:59.224
it's not over for me,

00:09:01.187 --> 00:09:03.590
I'm immediately going to
launch myself into training

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:04.877
for professional wrestling.

00:09:04.877 --> 00:09:07.804
I couldn't have launched myself
with that type of training

00:09:07.804 --> 00:09:11.472
that I did every day for hours
in a boxing ring that was

00:09:11.472 --> 00:09:15.546
(laughing) stiff like
concrete, every day for hours,

00:09:15.546 --> 00:09:19.009
I couldn't have launched myself
with that type of mentality

00:09:19.009 --> 00:09:20.631
had I not fallen on my face

00:09:20.631 --> 00:09:23.903
after 10 years of football failed.

00:09:23.903 --> 00:09:25.903
A failed football player

00:09:27.076 --> 00:09:30.895
who wound up moving back
in with his parents at 24.

00:09:30.895 --> 00:09:34.635
What inspires me in terms
of what I do in film or TV,

00:09:34.635 --> 00:09:38.509
or anything else, by the
way, any other platform,

00:09:38.509 --> 00:09:41.164
the first thing that comes
to mind when I read a script

00:09:41.164 --> 00:09:45.636
or a television project
that we're either producing,

00:09:45.636 --> 00:09:47.706
or in front of the camera,
or behind the camera is,

00:09:47.706 --> 00:09:48.829
will the audience like it?

00:09:48.829 --> 00:09:50.579
That is my number one

00:09:52.349 --> 00:09:54.016
thing I think about,

00:09:54.961 --> 00:09:56.319
is, will the audience like it?

00:09:56.319 --> 00:09:58.873
Will the audience like this
TV show that I'm putting out?

00:09:58.873 --> 00:10:01.285
For example, Ballers, will
the audience like Ballers?

00:10:01.285 --> 00:10:02.811
Will the audience like this role?

00:10:02.811 --> 00:10:06.978
Will the audience like this
movie, like with San Andreas?

00:10:07.928 --> 00:10:08.761
Will the audience like it?

00:10:08.761 --> 00:10:11.049
And then, so, that's part one.

00:10:11.049 --> 00:10:13.753
Part one B is, will I like it?

00:10:13.753 --> 00:10:15.257
Am I going to enjoy playing this?

00:10:15.257 --> 00:10:17.766
Because, look, cameras don't lie

00:10:17.766 --> 00:10:20.001
and as good as an actor

00:10:20.001 --> 00:10:21.399
as

00:10:21.399 --> 00:10:23.536
I am, or as I think I am, (laughs)

00:10:23.536 --> 00:10:25.795
if I didn't feel anything and
it wasn't coming from here,

00:10:25.795 --> 00:10:29.297
my heart, my soul, even if it's
a comedy or a dramatic role,

00:10:29.297 --> 00:10:31.271
action, whatever it is,

00:10:31.271 --> 00:10:32.384
TV,

00:10:32.384 --> 00:10:33.617
digital,

00:10:33.617 --> 00:10:34.957
Instagram, whatever,

00:10:34.957 --> 00:10:37.026
if I wasn't feeling it then

00:10:37.026 --> 00:10:38.728
you guys would be able to tell, so.

00:10:38.728 --> 00:10:40.616
Training, and some sort
of physical activity,

00:10:40.616 --> 00:10:42.237
becomes my anchor every day.

00:10:42.237 --> 00:10:43.392
To try and stay focused, for me,

00:10:43.392 --> 00:10:45.377
I've got to get up at 3:45 in the morning,

00:10:45.377 --> 00:10:47.323
I do my cardio, I'll have my breakfast,

00:10:47.323 --> 00:10:49.084
then I'll go train and hit the iron,

00:10:49.084 --> 00:10:50.115
then I'll go to set.

00:10:50.115 --> 00:10:52.025
And, if I can get those things
done before I go to set,

00:10:52.025 --> 00:10:54.690
then I can work for the
next 12, 14, 16 hours.

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:56.769
Fun part is the strategy behind it.

00:10:56.769 --> 00:10:58.591
Training for a role like Ballers

00:10:58.591 --> 00:11:01.138
is different than training
for a role like Baywatch,

00:11:01.138 --> 00:11:03.201
Fast and Furious, for a
role like San Andreas.

00:11:03.201 --> 00:11:05.031
They're all different
'cause if you do anything,

00:11:05.031 --> 00:11:06.906
you never want to do anything half-assed,

00:11:06.906 --> 00:11:08.186
especially when it comes to your training.

00:11:08.186 --> 00:11:11.133
Get in, be intense, execute
on it, then get out.

00:11:11.133 --> 00:11:12.206
For a lot of guys out there,

00:11:12.206 --> 00:11:13.433
and I was certainly one of those guys,

00:11:13.433 --> 00:11:16.127
in my 20's, I thought
I had all the answers.

00:11:16.127 --> 00:11:17.325
Didn't know (silent cursing), by the way.

00:11:17.325 --> 00:11:19.384
In my 30 years, I'm still
trying to find myself,

00:11:19.384 --> 00:11:20.963
as a lot of guys are out there.

00:11:20.963 --> 00:11:22.351
Hopefully when you hit your 40's,

00:11:22.351 --> 00:11:23.677
you're hitting a nice stride.

00:11:23.677 --> 00:11:26.193
Every day, today, I'm just more
appreciative of the moments.

00:11:26.193 --> 00:11:28.369
Been through relationships,
been through a divorce,

00:11:28.369 --> 00:11:30.598
have a beautiful daughter
and one on the way.

00:11:30.598 --> 00:11:31.431
Very, very lucky man.

00:11:31.431 --> 00:11:33.599
If I'm going to do it, I
need to do this job right,

00:11:33.599 --> 00:11:35.702
I need to stay focused on it
and I need to give the best

00:11:35.702 --> 00:11:36.927
effort I possibly can.

00:11:36.927 --> 00:11:39.161
And, not only that, but
just between us, I was like,

00:11:39.161 --> 00:11:40.838
I'm going to be the baddest
(silent cursing) walking

00:11:40.838 --> 00:11:42.671
at 43, 44, 45, 46, 47.

00:11:43.807 --> 00:11:45.240
- Thank you, guys, so much for watching.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:46.344
I hope you enjoyed.

00:11:46.344 --> 00:11:48.808
I'd love to know, what did
you take from this video

00:11:48.808 --> 00:11:51.422
that you're going to
immediately apply to your life

00:11:51.422 --> 00:11:52.481
or to your business?

00:11:52.481 --> 00:11:54.675
What was the most important
lesson that you learned?

00:11:54.675 --> 00:11:56.187
Please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:11:56.187 --> 00:11:57.605
I'm super curious to find out.

00:11:57.605 --> 00:11:59.371
I also want to give a
quick shout out to Charles.

00:11:59.371 --> 00:12:01.773
Charles, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:12:01.773 --> 00:12:03.764
Your One Word, and doing that review

00:12:03.764 --> 00:12:04.921
on your YouTube channel.

00:12:04.921 --> 00:12:06.061
I'm glad you enjoyed the book

00:12:06.061 --> 00:12:08.060
and I really, really
appreciate the support.

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:10.835
- Going to be reviewing
his book, Evan Carmichael,

00:12:10.835 --> 00:12:12.335
who is one of the,

00:12:13.263 --> 00:12:16.289
probably one of my original
YouTube subscribers.

00:12:16.289 --> 00:12:18.104
- So, thank you guys, again, for watching.

00:12:18.104 --> 00:12:19.474
I believe in you.

00:12:19.474 --> 00:12:21.485
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:12:21.485 --> 00:12:23.557
and whatever your one word is,

00:12:23.557 --> 00:12:24.851
much love.

00:12:24.851 --> 00:12:26.434
I'll see you soon.

